<section class="lp-section" id="qualified-meeting"><div class="lp-wrap">
  <div class="lp-heading"><h2>What counts as a qualified cybersecurity meeting?</h2><p>Check that the company fits your market and the contact can influence the decision. Record the security or recovery need and the next conversation the buyer agreed to.</p></div>
  <div class="shortlist-models">
    <article><h3>Security risk and governance</h3><p>For an assessment or compliance offer, find who owns the decision and ask what prompted the review. A deadline or an upcoming audit gives sales a reason to follow up.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Security operations</h3><p>For managed detection and response, talk to the person who owns coverage. Learn which responsibility they need help with and whether a vendor review is planned.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Infrastructure and recovery</h3><p>For disaster recovery or operational technology security, start with the team responsible for uptime. Ask how they review recovery readiness and who else should join the sales call.</p></article>
  </div>
  <div class="shortlist-feature"><h3>Give the caller a clear brief</h3><p>Give the caller an approved description of your offer and one customer example from that market. Use the <a href="/b2b-meeting-quality-check/">B2B meeting quality check</a> to agree on what sales should receive.</p></div>
  <div class="shortlist-example" id="pricing-example">
    <h3>Compare the bill with the meetings that happened</h3>
    <p>Belkins’ ten-meeting package starts at $9,000 including setup, or $900 per booking. If eight meetings take place and your sales team accepts six, that’s $1,500 per accepted meeting.</p>
    <p>Belkins uses email and LinkedIn and keeps no-shows in the package for rebooking.</p>
